📘 Notes: Understanding Observable Returned by GET
What Happens When You Call http.get()
Writing this.http.get(url) does not send a request immediately.
If you write it alone, without .subscribe(), no network call happens at all — check the Network tab, it will be empty.
http.get() only returns an Observable — it does not execute anything by itself.
What is an Observable
An Observable is an object that represents a stream of future values.
It is like a "plan" for getting data — but nothing happens until someone starts "listening" to it.
The actual HTTP request is only triggered when you call .subscribe() on it.
typescript
this.http.get('https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users')
  .subscribe((response) => {
    console.log(response);
  });
Only after .subscribe() is called:
The actual HTTP request is sent.
When the response arrives, the function inside subscribe() runs.
Real-Life Analogy — YouTube Channel
Finding a YouTube channel but not subscribing = creating the Observable (request not sent yet).
Subscribing to the channel = calling .subscribe() — now you start receiving notifications (data).
Until you subscribe, no notification (data) will ever reach you, even if the channel keeps uploading videos.
Observable vs Promise — Key Differences
Aspect	Promise	Observable
When it executes	Immediately, as soon as it's created	Only when .subscribe() is called
How many values it gives	Only once	Can give multiple values (a stream)
Can it be cancelled?	No	Yes, using .unsubscribe()
Angular's HttpClient.get() returns an Observable.
Plain JavaScript's fetch() returns a Promise.
This is exactly why in Angular, we always need to write .subscribe().
Step-by-Step Flow
this.http.get(url)
    ↓
An Observable object is created (but NO request is sent yet)
    ↓
.subscribe(...) is called
    ↓
NOW the actual HTTP request is triggered
    ↓
Server sends back a response
    ↓
The function inside subscribe() (next/error) runs
Why This Matters (Learning Outcome)
http.get() only creates an Observable — it does not send the request.
The request is only sent after .subscribe() is called.
Beginners often assume "I wrote http.get(), so the request went" — this is incorrect.
If you forget to write .subscribe(), there is no error — the request simply never goes. This is a silent bug that's easy to miss.
Practical Debugging Tip
If an API call seems to "not work" and there's no request visible in the Network tab, the first thing to check is: did you forget to write .subscribe()?
